#!/usr/bin/env sh

# Hairline-surface RATCHET + the `@apply glass-*` GATE.
#
# Two rules, one script (scripts/audit-hairline-surfaces.ts):
#
#   1. Hand-rolled card surfaces — `rounded-xl border t-border t-bg-subtle`
#      instead of the shared `.ios-card`. The liquid-glass sweep replaced the 1px
#      card border with a refracted rim, but it did that work INSIDE `.ios-card`,
#      so every component painting its own box kept the pre-glass look and
#      nothing flagged it. A GATE AT 0 since Phase 8: it landed as a ratchet at
#      26 because a gate at 0 before the sweep would just have been switched
#      off, the sweep is done, and this is now what keeps the remaining
#      `t-border` pile from growing back.
#      ⚠️ Never raise BASELINE to make a commit pass — that is the one rule.
#      A box that genuinely needs its own hairline gets tagged
#      `allow-hairline-surface`, with the reason, in a comment above it.
#
#   2. A `glass-*` class inside an `@apply`. These are plain CSS, not Tailwind
#      utilities: the dev server tolerates them and `pnpm build` fails with
#      "Cannot apply unknown utility class". A GATE from day one — there is no
#      legacy pile to drain, and every one is a broken production deploy that
#      only shows up on Vercel. This is why the check runs here rather than
#      living in someone's memory.
#
# Deliberately NOT `npm test` (husky's default), which would put a 30-second
# vitest run in front of every commit. CI already runs the suite.
pnpm audit:hairline-surfaces
